  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "How is an estate taxed when the beneficiary of an IRA is the estate?",
      "answer": "Traditional IRA withdrawals are taxable income reported through the estate on Form 1041, and the 2026 estate brackets reach 37% above $16,000 of taxable income. Whether the tax stays with the estate or passes out to the heirs depends on the facts, so ask a CPA or estate attorney with the estate's numbers."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can an estate be the beneficiary of an IRA?",
      "answer": "Yes. An owner can name the estate on the form, and the estate can also end up as beneficiary when no form is on file or the named beneficiary died first. Because an estate is not an individual, it cannot be a designated beneficiary under [IRS Publication 590-B](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b)."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the 5-year rule for an estate-owned inherited IRA?",
      "answer": "If the owner died before the required beginning date, the estate must empty the IRA by December 31 of the year containing the fifth anniversary of the death. No withdrawals are required in years one through four. Any balance left after the deadline is subject to the excise tax for insufficient distributions."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does the 10-year rule apply to an estate?",
      "answer": "No. The 10-year rule applies to designated beneficiaries, who are individuals, and an estate is not one. An estate uses the 5-year rule if the owner died before the required beginning date, or the owner's remaining life expectancy if death came on or after it."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is an IRA subject to probate?",
      "answer": "IRS Publication 590-B does not answer the probate question. What is settled is that a beneficiary designation on an IRA overrides the will, and when the estate is the beneficiary, the account is paid to the estate. Our [probate guide](/probate/) explains the probate process, and an estate attorney can tell you how your state treats the account."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can the estate take the IRA as a lump sum?",
      "answer": "Yes, under the 5-year rule, because withdrawals can follow any schedule as long as the account is empty by the deadline. One $100,000 withdrawal taxed to the estate in 2026 would owe $34,931, while five $20,000 withdrawals would owe $26,655 in total if 2026 rates held. For the life-expectancy method, [Publication 590-B](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b) sets the payout period."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does a Roth IRA left to an estate follow the same rules?",
      "answer": "Yes, for timing. A Roth IRA inherited by an estate follows the same 5-year and life-expectancy payout clocks as a traditional IRA, but qualified Roth distributions are not taxed."
    }
  ],
